flowdocks (0.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Pin folders, drives and files. Drag one from a file manager onto the dock,
    or use "Pin a folder..." / "Pin a file..." in the dock menu. A file's menu
    also offers "Open containing folder". A pinned drive drops off the dock
    while it is unmounted and returns when it is mounted again.
  * Clicking a pinned folder or drive opens it in the file manager -- the
    running desktop's own first, then any installed one, then the freedesktop
    file-manager D-Bus service -- rather than following the inode/directory
    MIME default, which distros and editors such as VS Code often claim.
  * Refresh the application catalogue automatically. It is watched for changes
    and rescanned on drag, so an app installed since the dock started -- a
    Chrome/Chromium "install as app" launcher such as Google Gemini, for
    instance -- can be pinned without restarting. A drop that still cannot be
    matched now reports why instead of being ignored silently.

 -- PrismoVector <info@prismovector.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 21:30:00 +0000

flowdocks (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Relicense from MIT to a proprietary all-rights-reserved licence. The source
    remains published to be read, but no right to use, copy, modify or
    redistribute it is granted; installing and running an official release
    stays permitted for personal use.
  * Reconcile the contributing and installation guidance with the new terms.

 -- PrismoVector <info@prismovector.com>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0000

flowdocks (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add separators. A pinned slot can hold a divider instead of an application,
    grouping icons the way Nexus Dock does. Add one from the dock menu, drag it
    to reposition it, and drag it off the dock or use its menu to remove it.
    Separators are addressed by position, so identical ones stay distinct.
  * Support up to five docks at once. Each keeps its own pinned apps, edge,
    orientation, theme, size and layer, and is added or removed from the Docks
    submenu. The global shortcut and the --toggle and --preferences commands
    act on all of them together.
  * Settings files written by earlier versions are read as a single dock, and
    the shortcut is lifted out of it, so nothing is lost on upgrade.

 -- PrismoVector <info@prismovector.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 23:55:00 +0000

flowdocks (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Let a free-positioned dock reach the screen edges. Free positioning now
    places the visible shelf rather than its padded window, and moves that
    padding to whichever side has room, so the dock sits flush against an edge
    instead of stopping a tooltip's height short of it.
  * Add a horizontal/vertical orientation setting for free positioning, so the
    dock can be rotated into a column. A docked dock still takes its orientation
    from the edge it is attached to.
  * Replace the combo box arrows in preferences with a painted chevron, and
    restyle the drop-down lists to match the rest of the dialog.

 -- PrismoVector <info@prismovector.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 22:30:00 +0000

flowdocks (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rename the project to FlowDocks. The package, command, icon, manual page and
    configuration directory all move to that name; settings and pinned apps are
    read once from the previous configuration directory so nothing is lost.
  * Accept an application dropped from the desktop, the file manager or the
    applications menu. A dropped launcher is matched to the installed app by
    path, desktop-file ID and finally its Exec line, so a copy or symlink of an
    installed entry now pins correctly instead of being rejected.
  * Add a free positioning mode: the dock can be dragged anywhere on the screen
    instead of only snapping to an edge. Auto-hide and the edge hotspot are
    disabled in that mode, leaving the shortcut, tray and menu to hide it.
  * Add controls for the desktop's own panel, in the menu and in preferences:
    hide or show it, move it to any edge, and open its settings. XFCE is driven
    through xfconf; MATE and Cinnamon through their GSettings keys; LXQt, KDE,
    GNOME and Budgie offer their settings dialog only. Unsupported actions are
    greyed out and explained rather than failing silently.

 -- PrismoVector <info@prismovector.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 21:00:00 +0000

flowdocks (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Released under the project's former name.
  * Default to the normal window layer so other windows can cover the dock, and
    add Above and Below layer choices in preferences and the context menu.
  * Add a global show/hide shortcut (Ctrl+Alt+A by default), configurable or
    disabled in preferences, plus --toggle and --preferences commands that talk
    to the running dock over a per-user, per-display socket.
  * Make the screen edge hotspot configurable: reveal, toggle or disabled.
  * Allow dragging icons from the application picker onto the dock, dragging
    pinned icons to reorder them, and dragging an icon off the dock to unpin it.
  * Allow dragging the dock itself by its grip, clock or empty surface to
    snap it to any screen edge on any monitor, with an optional position lock.
  * Add a no-root, architecture-independent Debian package with an explicit
    runtime file list, desktop actions, scalable icon, manual page and local
    documentation.
  * Record unspecified project licensing; public distribution requires the
    owner's confirmation of distribution rights.

 -- PrismoVector <info@prismovector.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
